\f1\b\fs28 Request\f0\b0\fs24 \par
Hi team - before I sign off the Q4 cross-sell budget I need proof that targeting beats our random contacts. Can you use the 30 June customer snapshot (we now have the July-September outcomes) to build a model of who takes up another product and why, and show me which 4,000 customers it would have picked and how many of them actually converted versus random? Remember we can only contact opted-in customers and gender can't be used. Need it for the 15 October planning meeting - brief and data dictionary are in the folder.\par
\par
\f1\b\fs28 Task Description\f0\b0\fs24 \par
1. Review the campaign brief (Word) and data dictionary (Excel) to capture budget (4,000 contacts), unit economics ($4 per contact, $150 margin), compliance rules (opt-in only, no gender, exclude customers with 2+ complaints) and acceptance criteria; flag columns that may be populated after the purchase.\par
2. In a VS Code Jupyter notebook, profile the 20,000-customer snapshot and fix data quality issues: CRM-merge duplicates, inconsistent region and product labels, sentinel ages and balances, negative tenure, mixed date formats, and non-random missing income; save the cleaned dataset (artifact 1).\par
3. Audit for leakage, confirm and remove post-outcome fields (welcome_pack_sent, cross_sell_product_type), exclude gender, de-duplicate before a stratified 80/20 train/test split.\par
4. Train a logistic-regression baseline, then an improved HistGradientBoosting model; evaluate on the hold-out with ROC-AUC, PR-AUC against the 11.6% base rate, decile lift and a calibration curve; save the model comparison (artifact 2).\par
5. Explain purchase drivers with SHAP and translate them into messaging guidance for Marketing.\par
6. Back-test: score every customer with 5-fold out-of-fold predictions, apply compliance and customer-care filters, select the top 4,000 (artifact 3), and compare expected vs actual conversions and ROI against random selection in Excel (final output 2).\par
7. Package the final model, metrics and README for scoring the 30 September snapshot (final output 3), confirm metrics reproduce after Restart Kernel and Run All, and build a 3-slide summary in PowerPoint (final output 4).\par
Validation criteria: hold-out ROC-AUC >= 0.78; beats the logistic baseline on PR-AUC; top-20% lift >= 2.5x; no leaky or protected fields used; back-test list has exactly 4,000 compliant customers converting at >= 2x the eligible average; metrics reproducible.\par
}
